WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.240
<v Instructor>So now let's talk about AWS DataSync.</v>

2
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.730
And DataSync is a service that is appearing

3
00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.950
now quite a lot at the exam.

4
00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:08.880
It's a very simple one,

5
00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.100
but you need to know what it does at its core.

6
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:15.210
So the idea, as the name indicates, is to synchronize data.

7
00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:19.440
Therefore, move large amount of data to and from places.

8
00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.720
And these places can be, for example,

9
00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.180
on-premises or other cloud locations into AWS.

10
00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:29.640
So you would connect to your server using the NFS,

11
00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:33.510
the SMB, the HDFS, or other protocols.

12
00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.210
And it needs an agent to run on-premises

13
00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:38.940
or on the other cloud to do that connection.

14
00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.910
And you can, for example, do other types of migrations.

15
00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:44.967
For example, you can move data from one

16
00:00:44.967 --> 00:00:48.180
AWS service to another AWS service.

17
00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.980
And this requires no agent.

18
00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.270
I will show you what it means.

19
00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:55.830
So the idea is that you can synchronize data to Amazon S3,

20
00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:58.020
including any storage classes,

21
00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.050
even Glacier, Amazon EFS to store

22
00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.690
into your network file system,

23
00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:07.260
or Amazon FSx, and it supports all of them.

24
00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.930
Now, the replication tasks are not continuous.

25
00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:11.370
They are scheduled.

26
00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:15.870
So you can make DataSync run hourly, daily, or weekly.

27
00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:17.280
So there's a lag, okay,

28
00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.820
but the data is going to be synchronized on a schedule.

29
00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.000
On top of it,

30
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.050
DataSync has the ability to keep

31
00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.510
the file permissions and the metadata.

32
00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:29.910
That means the security and so on.

33
00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:31.900
So that means that it's compliant with the NFS

34
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:35.940
POSIX file system and the SMB permissions.

35
00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.460
This is very important because at the exam,

36
00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.470
this will be the unique option that will

37
00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:42.100
preserve the metadata of your file

38
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.630
when moving them from one location to another.

39
00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:47.540
One DataSync agent can be quite powerful.

40
00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:50.160
It can run one task,

41
00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.550
can use up to 10 gigabits of data per second.

42
00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:56.370
Although if you don't want to max out your network,

43
00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:58.360
you can set up a bandwidth limit.

44
00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:01.920
So let's have a look at what it means in a diagram.

45
00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.280
So here is the use case of synchronizing

46
00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:07.320
your on-premises files using the SMB

47
00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.170
or NFS protocol into AWS,

48
00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:12.540
and that could be S3, EFS, or FSx.

49
00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:14.370
So you have your on-premises and then

50
00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:17.760
your AWS region where DataSync is running.

51
00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.910
So here is your NFS or SMB server.

52
00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:22.260
And what you have to do is to

53
00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.880
install on-premises the AWS DataSync agent.

54
00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:31.200
And you will tell it to connect to your NFS or SMB server.

55
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.040
And then the DataSync agent will establish a connection

56
00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:35.250
and also connect in an encrypted

57
00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:37.950
fashion into the DataSync service.

58
00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.710
From there, you can tell it to go wherever you want.

59
00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:44.420
That could be any storage class for your Amazon S3 buckets,

60
00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:49.420
or it could be AWS EFS, or it could be Amazon FSx.

61
00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:51.960
And the synchronization can happen one way

62
00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.660
from on-premises to AWS,

63
00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.940
but you can also synchronize from

64
00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:00.270
AWS back into on-premises.

65
00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:01.680
This is why it's called DataSync.

66
00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:02.823
It can work any way.

67
00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.000
So that is to show the architecture

68
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.750
of synchronization from on-premises to AWS,

69
00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:11.220
or it could be, for example,

70
00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:14.580
another cloud to AWS using the DataSync agent.

71
00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.680
But you can also use DataSync to just

72
00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.770
synchronize between different AWS storage services.

73
00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:22.000
For example, you wanna synchronize between Amazon S3

74
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.780
or Amazon EFS or Amazon FSx

75
00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:29.460
back into Amazon S3, Amazon EFS, or Amazon FSx.

76
00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:34.460
And for this, again, we will use the AWS DataSync service.

77
00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.990
And it will copy the data, of course,

78
00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:38.820
but also the metadata will be kept

79
00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:41.580
between the different AWS storage services,

80
00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:42.570
which is very important.

81
00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:45.060
And again, something that can come up in the exam.

82
00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:46.440
So to remind you,

83
00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:50.070
DataSync can pretty much synchronize between anything,

84
00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:51.480
but it is not continuous.

85
00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:54.360
It is scheduled tasks that can be happening hourly,

86
00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.430
daily, weekly.

87
00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:58.440
And also, it will preserve metadata

88
00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:01.830
and your file permissions.

89
00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:04.980
And finally, you need to run the DataSync agent

90
00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:08.790
if you are connecting to an NFS or SMB server.

91
00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:10.530
Okay, that's it for this lecture.

92
00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:11.460
I hope you liked it.

93
00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.410
And I will see you in the next lecture.

